# A recorded session over a fixed world. Every frame this prints is a
# function of this directory alone, so two builds -- or two implementations
# -- can be compared with `diff`.
#
# The schedule, shared by every fixture here: one real slice, many sessions.
cache ../cache.db
# The Friday this fixture's service runs, at a time with departures ahead.
date 2026-09-11
time 08:00
# Ottawa on that date. Named, so the frames do not move with the reader.
offset -04:00
size 100 14

# The pin is row 0: its board, entered and left again. `esc` from a pin must
# land on the first screen, not part-way up a drill path nobody walked.
enter
esc

# Bus -> 44 -> a direction -> a stop -> its board. The detour rides along
# from the route screen down, and 44 and 48 share this platform.
#
# The direction is named, not taken by position. A bare `enter` took whichever
# direction sorted first, and adding trips to the slice moved it, so the board
# underneath silently became a different board and the fixture stopped reaching
# the row it was recorded for.
down
enter
type 44
enter
type hurdman
enter
enter

# Back out to the top one level at a time.
esc
esc
esc
esc

# The O-Train: rail has no realtime at all, so every row must read `sched`.
#
# The direction is named here for the same reason it is named on the bus walk
# above. Rail was left on a bare enter, and a tiebreak added to the direction
# order moved it to the other end of the line.
down
down
enter
enter
type tunney
enter
enter
esc
esc
esc
esc

# Search finds a stop by name, and its board mixes routes. Typing goes straight
# to the search here, so this walk does not press `/` first. `pin-onward` is
# where `/` earns its step, because there it jumps from a board.
type terminal
enter
